<br class="ltx_break"><span id="id1.1.1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">AI Lab, Grupo Santander
<br class="ltx_break">Madrid, Spain</span></span></span>
</span></span>
<span id="id1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">
<span id="id1.1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_top" style="width:144.9pt;">
<span id="id1.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">Juan Manuel Vera Díaz <a href="https://orcid.org/0000-0002-6152-5789" title="ORCID 0000-0002-6152-5789" class="ltx_ref ltx_orcid" style="font-size:90%;"> <svg class="ltx_orcidlogo" height="1em" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 72 72" width="1em"><path style="--ltx-fill-color:#A6CE39;" d="M72,36 C72,55.884375 55.884375,72 36,72 C16.115625,72 0,55.884375 0,36 C0,16.115625 16.115625,0 36,0 C55.884375,0 72,16.115625 72,36 Z" fill="#A6CE39"></path><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#FFFFFF;" fill="#FFFFFF" transform="translate(18.868966, 12.910345)"><polygon points="5.03734929 39.1250878 0.695429861 39.1250878 0.695429861 9.14431787 5.03734929 9.14431787 5.03734929 22.6930505 5.03734929 39.1250878"></polygon><path d="M11.409257,9.14431787 L23.1380784,9.14431787 C34.303014,9.14431787 39.2088191,17.0664074 39.2088191,24.1486995 C39.2088191,31.846843 33.1470485,39.1530811 23.1944669,39.1530811 L11.409257,39.1530811 L11.409257,9.14431787 Z M15.7511765,35.2620194 L22.6587756,35.2620194 C32.49858,35.2620194 34.7541226,27.8438084 34.7541226,24.1486995 C34.7541226,18.1301509 30.8915059,13.0353795 22.4332213,13.0353795 L15.7511765,13.0353795 L15.7511765,35.2620194 Z"></path><path d="M5.71401206,2.90182329 C5.71401206,4.441452 4.44526937,5.72914146 2.86638958,5.72914146 C1.28750978,5.72914146 0.0187670918,4.441452 0.0187670918,2.90182329 C0.0187670918,1.33420133 1.28750978,0.0745051096 2.86638958,0.0745051096 C4.44526937,0.0745051096 5.71401206,1.36219458 5.71401206,2.90182329 Z"></path></g></svg></a></span>
<span id="id1.1.2.1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="id1.1.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Senior AI Researcher
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="id1.1.2.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">AI Lab, Grupo Santander
<br class="ltx_break">Madrid, Spain</span></span></span>
</span></span></span>
</span>
</span>
</span><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span><span id="id2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Corresponding author:
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="id2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">juanma.vera@gruposantander.com</span></span></span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Selection as Power argued that upstream selection authority, rather than internal objective misalignment, constitutes a primary source of risk in high-stakes agentic systems. However, the original framework was static: governance constraints bounded selection power but did not adapt over time. In this work, we extend the framework to dynamic settings by introducing incentivized selection governance, where reinforcement updates are applied to scoring and reducer parameters under externally enforced sovereignty constraints.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.2" class="ltx_p">We formalize selection as a constrained reinforcement process in which parameter updates are projected onto governance-defined feasible sets, preventing concentration beyond prescribed bounds. Across multiple regulated financial scenarios, unconstrained reinforcement consistently collapses into deterministic dominance under repeated feedback, especially at higher learning rates. In contrast, incentivized governance enables adaptive improvement while maintaining bounded selection concentration.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.3" class="ltx_p">Projection-based constraints transform reinforcement from irreversible lock-in into controlled adaptation, with governance debt quantifying the tension between optimization pressure and authority bounds. These results demonstrate that learning dynamics can coexist with structural diversity when sovereignty constraints are enforced at every update step, offering a principled approach to integrating reinforcement into high-stakes agentic systems without surrendering bounded selection authority.</p>
    
<p id="abstract1.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="abstract1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Keywords:</span>
<span id="abstract1.4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Constrained Reinforcement Learning, Mechanism Design for AI Systems, Multi-Agent Learning, Adversarial Robustness, AI Governance</span></p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">I </span><span id="S1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Introduction</span></h2>

<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S1.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">I-A</span> </span><span id="S1.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">From Static Selection Governance to Adaptive Incentives</span></h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Autonomous agentic systems are increasingly deployed in regulated, high-stakes domains where decisions are irreversible, externally audited, and institutionally constrained. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, we argued that the primary locus of risk in such systems is not merely what agents compute, but what they are allowed to select, suppress, and present. We introduced a governance architecture that mechanically bounds selection authority by separating cognition, selection, and action into distinct domains with externally enforced constraints.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">That framework positioned itself alongside alignment-based safety approaches such as reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, constitutional AI <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, and constrained reinforcement learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. While these approaches shape internal objectives or restrict action execution, institutional analyses of algorithmic accountability have shown that alignment and transparency alone do not eliminate structural power embedded in upstream selection mechanisms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The original governance architecture demonstrated that selection power can be bounded through architectural separation and mechanical enforcement. However, it was intentionally static: governance parameters were fixed, and the system did not adapt based on feedback over time. While mechanical governance prevents deterministic capture of outcomes, it does not allow reinforcement of correct selections nor improvement under repeated interaction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This paper addresses that limitation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S1.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">I-B</span> </span><span id="S1.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The Central Question</span></h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We investigate whether selection governance can be made <em id="S1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">adaptive</em> without reintroducing concentration of power. Specifically, we ask:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<blockquote id="S1.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_quote">
<p id="S1.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_p">Can a governance architecture incorporate incentive mechanisms that reinforce correct selections over time, while preserving bounded selection authority under adversarial and multi-agent conditions?</p>
</blockquote>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Incentive mechanisms are foundational in modern machine learning. Reinforcement learning provides a principled framework for improving decision policies through feedback <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, and multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) studies how interacting agents adapt under shared or competing objectives <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. However, unconstrained reinforcement can amplify selection concentration, induce feedback loops, and create collusive equilibria among agents optimizing jointly for reward <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In governance-sensitive environments, such dynamics risk reintroducing precisely the concentration of power that static selection constraints were designed to prevent.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S1.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">I-C</span> </span><span id="S1.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Incentivized Selection Governance</span></h3>

<div id="S1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To address this challenge, we introduce <em id="S1.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Selection as Power 2.0</em>: an incentivized governance framework in which both the scoring agent and the selection reducer are updated via feedback signals, but only within externally enforced sovereignty constraints.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">At a high level, the system operates as follows. A governed selection mechanism chooses an agent to act under a given context. The chosen agent produces an output, which is evaluated by a perfect evaluator that provides binary correctness feedback. That feedback is then used to update both scoring preferences and selection parameters. Crucially, all updates are constrained by governance rules that prevent the system from collapsing into deterministic dominance or eliminating structural diversity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This transforms selection governance from a static bounding mechanism into a dynamic constrained learning process.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S1.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">I-D</span> </span><span id="S1.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Contributions</span></h3>

<div id="S1.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper makes five contributions:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S1.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We formalize incentivized selection governance as a reinforcement process operating under externally enforced sovereignty constraints.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce dual-update learning for both scoring and reducer parameters while preserving mechanical enforcement.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We provide theoretical analysis showing that constrained updates can bound selection concentration under iterative adaptation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We empirically evaluate the framework in regulated financial scenarios, measuring stability, and governance debt across temporal dynamics.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S1.SS5.6" class="ltx_text">I-E</span> </span><span id="S1.SS5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Roadmap</span></h3>

<div id="S1.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The remainder of the paper proceeds as follows. Section <a href="#S2" title="II Related Work ‣ Selection as Power: Constrained Reinforcement for Bounded Decision Authority" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a> situates this work within reinforcement learning, mechanism design, and multi-agent systems. Section <a href="#S3" title="III Problem Setting: Incentivized Selection Governance ‣ Selection as Power: Constrained Reinforcement for Bounded Decision Authority" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a> formalizes the incentivized governance model. Section <a href="#S4" title="IV Architecture for Incentivized Selection Governance ‣ Selection as Power: Constrained Reinforcement for Bounded Decision Authority" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV</span></a> presents the constrained reinforcement architecture. Section <a href="#S5" title="V Experimental Setup ‣ Selection as Power: Constrained Reinforcement for Bounded Decision Authority" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a> describes the experimental setup. Section <a href="#S6" title="VI Experimental Results ‣ Selection as Power: Constrained Reinforcement for Bounded Decision Authority" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VI</span></a> reports empirical findings. Section <a href="#S7" title="VII Discussion ‣ Selection as Power: Constrained Reinforcement for Bounded Decision Authority" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VII</span></a> analyzes stability and dynamics. Section <a href="#S8" title="VIII Limitations and Future Work ‣ Selection as Power: Constrained Reinforcement for Bounded Decision Authority" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">VIII</span></a> discusses limitations and future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">II </span><span id="S2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Related Work</span></h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This work lies at the intersection of reinforcement learning, mechanism design, multi-agent systems, and AI governance. While each of these areas addresses aspects of adaptation and control, none directly formalizes incentivized selection governance under externally enforced sovereignty constraints.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">II-A</span> </span><span id="S2.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Reinforcement Learning and Constrained Optimization</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reinforcement learning (RL) provides a general framework for optimizing sequential decision policies under feedback <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>. Policy gradient methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> and actor-critic architectures are widely used in large-scale systems. However, unconstrained RL may lead to unintended behaviors when reward signals are incomplete or misspecified.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Constrained reinforcement learning addresses this issue by incorporating explicit safety constraints, often modeled via constrained Markov decision processes (CMDPs) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite>. Shielding methods introduce runtime safety filters that block unsafe actions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>. While these approaches restrict action execution, they typically assume a fixed action space and do not explicitly govern upstream selection authority.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our work differs in two ways. First, we treat selection as a governed resource rather than a fixed action space. Second, reinforcement updates are projected onto externally defined sovereignty constraint sets, ensuring that adaptation does not erode structural governance guarantees.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">II-B</span> </span><span id="S2.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Reward Modeling and Alignment</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reward modeling and reinforcement learning from human feedback (RLHF) have become central to aligning large models with human preferences <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>. Constitutional AI further constrains behavior through rule-based critique and self-improvement <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These approaches focus primarily on aligning internal objectives. However, as argued in algorithmic accountability research <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite> and in incomplete contracting analyses of AI alignment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>]</cite>, internal alignment does not eliminate structural asymmetries in decision authority. An aligned agent may still exercise disproportionate influence through upstream selection or framing.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our framework complements alignment-based approaches by governing how reinforcement reshapes selection authority over time, rather than solely shaping internal preferences.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">II-C</span> </span><span id="S2.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Multi-agent reinforcement learning (MARL) studies how interacting agents adapt under shared or competing objectives <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>. Cooperative and competitive equilibria can emerge even without explicit communication, particularly in repeated settings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Game-theoretic analyses of repeated interaction demonstrate that collusive equilibria can arise under learning dynamics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>]</cite>. In MARL settings, agents may implicitly coordinate to maximize joint reward, leading to emergent cooperative strategies that may not align with system-level objectives.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our work extends selection governance to explicitly account for multi-agent dynamics. We introduce a formal measure of agent selection and analyze whether constrained reinforcement can bound amplification of selection probabilities.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">II-D</span> </span><span id="S2.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Mechanism Design and Incentive Compatibility</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Mechanism design provides formal tools for structuring incentives in strategic environments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>. In algorithmic settings, mechanism design principles have been applied to auctions, allocation rules, and distributed systems.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, classical mechanism design assumes agents act within predefined allocation rules. In contrast, agentic AI systems can influence the construction of the option space itself. Selection governance therefore requires bounding not only incentives but also authority over option generation and reduction.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our framework can be interpreted as a constrained mechanism in which reinforcement updates are treated as strategic moves projected onto governance-preserving constraint sets.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS5.6" class="ltx_text">II-E</span> </span><span id="S2.SS5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">AI Governance and Accountability</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Institutional analyses emphasize that safety in high-stakes systems requires enforceable constraints rather than purely normative guidelines <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. Governance mechanisms must operate causally, not merely epistemically.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our previous work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite> formalized this idea for static systems. The present work extends it to dynamic settings in which incentives reshape decision policies over time. The key contribution is to show that adaptive reinforcement can coexist with bounded selection authority, provided sovereignty constraints remain externally enforced.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S2.SS6.6" class="ltx_text">II-F</span> </span><span id="S2.SS6.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Positioning of This Work</span></h3>

<div id="S2.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In summary:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike standard RL, we govern selection rather than only action.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike constrained RL, we bound reinforcement updates via sovereignty projection.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike RLHF, we regulate structural authority rather than internal preference alignment.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike classical MARL, we explicitly model and bound selection dynamics.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To our knowledge, this is the first framework to integrate constrained reinforcement, selection governance, and multi-agent selection analysis into a unified architecture for high-stakes agentic systems.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">III </span><span id="S3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Problem Setting: Incentivized Selection Governance</span></h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We formalize the dynamic extension of Selection as Power introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. The setting extends the static governance architecture to a sequential learning framework in which selection policies and reducer parameters are updated based on evaluative feedback, while remaining subject to externally enforced sovereignty constraints.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-A</span> </span><span id="S3.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">System Overview</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We consider a discrete-time process indexed by <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1,\dots,T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1,\dots,T</annotation></semantics></math>. At each time step:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A task context <math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is presented.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A governed selection mechanism chooses an agent <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> from a finite candidate set <math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}=\{A_{1},\dots,A_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}=\{A_{1},\dots,A_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The selected agent produces an output <math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{t}=A_{i}(x_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{t}=A_{i}(x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A perfect evaluator <math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> returns a correctness signal <math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}\in\{-1,+1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}\in\{-1,+1\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Selection and reducer parameters are updated using <math id="S3.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, subject to governance constraints.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The evaluator is assumed to be correct and deterministic. We therefore abstract away uncertainty in reward labeling and focus exclusively on selection dynamics.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Candidate Agents and Context</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> denote a finite set of candidate agents. Each agent <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> maps contexts to outputs:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathcal{O}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}:\mathcal{X}\rightarrow\mathcal{O}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The task context <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}\in\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}\in\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> is drawn from an exogenous distribution <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math>. We assume <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> is stationary over the evaluation horizon.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike classical reinforcement learning settings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, agents do not directly optimize over actions. Instead, the system optimizes over <em id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">which agent is selected</em> to act under the given context.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span> </span><span id="S3.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Governed Selection Policy</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At time <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the system maintains selection parameters <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. These parameters define a selection policy:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta_{t}}(A_{i}\mid x_{t})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta_{t}}(A_{i}\mid x_{t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">which determines the probability of selecting agent <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> under context <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Crucially, <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta_{t}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta_{t}}</annotation></semantics></math> is not unconstrained. It must satisfy externally imposed sovereignty constraints that bound selection authority.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">III-D</span> </span><span id="S3.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Reducer Parameters</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to selection weights, the system maintains reducer parameters <math id="S3.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. These include quantities such as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">diversity quotas,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">threshold values,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">exploration coefficients,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">variance bounds.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Reducer parameters determine how scored candidates are filtered and surfaced, following the architectural structure defined in the prior work on Selection as Power <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS5.6" class="ltx_text">III-E</span> </span><span id="S3.SS5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Sovereignty Constraint Sets</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We formalize governance constraints as closed feasible sets:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t}\in\mathcal{C},\qquad\phi_{t}\in\mathcal{G}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t}\in\mathcal{C},\qquad\phi_{t}\in\mathcal{G}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> constrains selection parameters.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> constrains reducer parameters.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">These sets encode invariants such as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p5" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">minimum exploration probability,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">bounded selection concentration,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">diversity requirements,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">entropy isolation constraints.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS5.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Importantly, the constraint sets are externally defined and not modifiable by the learning process.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS6.6" class="ltx_text">III-F</span> </span><span id="S3.SS6.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Reinforcement Updates</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After observing reward <math id="S3.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the system updates parameters.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS6.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Selection Update.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Selection parameters are updated via projected gradient ascent:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t+1}=\Pi_{\mathcal{C}}\!\left(\theta_{t}+\alpha r_{t}\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta_{t}}(A_{i}\mid x_{t})\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t+1}=\Pi_{\mathcal{C}}\!\left(\theta_{t}+\alpha r_{t}\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta_{t}}(A_{i}\mid x_{t})\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{\mathcal{C}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{\mathcal{C}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes projection onto the feasible set <math id="S3.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is a learning rate.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS6.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reducer Update.</h5>

<div id="S3.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Reducer parameters are updated analogously:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t+1}=\Pi_{\mathcal{G}}\!\left(\phi_{t}+\beta r_{t}\nabla_{\phi}L_{\text{selection}}(\phi_{t})\right)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>selection</mtext></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t+1}=\Pi_{\mathcal{G}}\!\left(\phi_{t}+\beta r_{t}\nabla_{\phi}L_{\text{selection}}(\phi_{t})\right),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{\text{selection}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mtext>selection</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{\text{selection}}</annotation></semantics></math> captures a differentiable surrogate of selection quality and <math id="S3.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is a learning rate.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Projection-based updates are standard in constrained optimization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>. Here they enforce sovereignty constraints at every iteration.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS7.6" class="ltx_text">III-G</span> </span><span id="S3.SS7.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Selection Concentration</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To quantify selection authority, we define the empirical inclusion probability of agent <math id="S3.SS7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{t}(A_{i})=\mathbb{E}[\pi_{\theta_{t}}(A_{i}\mid x_{t})]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{t}(A_{i})=\mathbb{E}[\pi_{\theta_{t}}(A_{i}\mid x_{t})].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS7.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We define <em id="S3.SS7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Selection Concentration</em> at time <math id="S3.SS7.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{SC}_{t}=\max_{i}P_{t}(A_{i})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mtext>SC</mtext><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>i</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{SC}_{t}=\max_{i}P_{t}(A_{i}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS7.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Bounded selection power requires:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sup_{t}\text{SC}_{t}\leq\gamma," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">sup</mo><mi>t</mi></munder><msub><mtext>SC</mtext><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sup_{t}\text{SC}_{t}\leq\gamma,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS7.p3.2" class="ltx_p">for some <math id="S3.SS7.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma&lt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma&lt;1</annotation></semantics></math> determined by governance constraints.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS8" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS8.6" class="ltx_text">III-H</span> </span><span id="S3.SS8.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Stability Objective</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike classical RL, we do not require convergence to an optimal fixed point. Instead, we require empirical stability:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I5" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">bounded selection concentration,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">bounded variance of <math id="S3.I5.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{SC}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>SC</mtext><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{SC}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS8.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Formally, we define empirical stability as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS8.p4" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\limsup_{t\to T}\text{SC}_{t}\leq\gamma." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">lim sup</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></munder><msub><mtext>SC</mtext><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>γ</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\limsup_{t\to T}\text{SC}_{t}\leq\gamma.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS8.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS8.p5.1" class="ltx_p">This defines adaptive governance as a constrained dynamical system rather than an unconstrained optimization process.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS9" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS9.6" class="ltx_text">III-I</span> </span><span id="S3.SS9.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Comparison with Standard RL</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In standard reinforcement learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>]</cite>, policy updates are unconstrained except for optional regularization. In contrast, our model enforces projection onto governance constraint sets at each iteration, structurally bounding authority even under sustained positive reinforcement.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS9.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS9.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The remainder of the paper analyzes the implications of this constrained dynamical system under adversarial and collusive conditions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Architecture for Incentivized Selection Governance</span></h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section presents the system architecture that instantiates the formal model introduced in Section <a href="#S3" title="III Problem Setting: Incentivized Selection Governance ‣ Selection as Power: Constrained Reinforcement for Bounded Decision Authority" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>. The objective is to integrate reinforcement-based adaptation into the original Selection as Power architecture while preserving externally enforced sovereignty constraints.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The architecture extends the static pipeline (CEFL <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> Scoring <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> Reducer <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> Presentation Gate) by introducing an evaluative feedback loop and constrained parameter updates. The resulting system can be interpreted as a constrained dynamical mechanism rather than a fixed decision pipeline.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Architectural Overview</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At each time step <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the system executes the following stages:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Candidate Expansion (CEFL).</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scoring and Feature Evaluation.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Governed Reduction and Selection.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output Execution.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation and Reward Assignment.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">6.</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Constrained Parameter Updates.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ IV-A Architectural Overview ‣ IV Architecture for Incentivized Selection Governance ‣ Selection as Power: Constrained Reinforcement for Bounded Decision Authority" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes the full iterative procedure of the incentivized selection governance architecture. The algorithm makes explicit the separation between governed candidate generation, adaptive scoring, reducer-based filtering, evaluation, and constrained parameter updates. Importantly, reinforcement signals affect both scoring and reducer parameters, but every update is projected onto externally defined sovereignty constraint sets. This projection step is the architectural safeguard that distinguishes incentivized governance from unconstrained reinforcement learning: adaptation occurs, but only within a mechanically enforced feasible region. The procedure also incorporates fail-loud mechanisms, ensuring that strategic coordination or excessive concentration triggers corrective intervention rather than silent drift.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Incentivized Selection Governance (Iterative Procedure)</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algx1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algx1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
              
              
            Candidate set <math id="algx1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math>, initial selection parameters <math id="algx1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{0}\in\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{0}\in\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, reducer parameters <math id="algx1.l1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{0}\in\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{0}\in\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, evaluator <math id="algx1.l1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math>, learning rates <math id="algx1.l1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha,\beta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha,\beta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, horizon <math id="algx1.l1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algx1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
              
              
            Audit log <math id="algx1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algx1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
              
              
            <math id="algx1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}\leftarrow\varnothing" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}\leftarrow\varnothing</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algx1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="algx1.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="algx1.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="algx1.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="algx1.l4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algx1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="algx1.l5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Context:</span> Receive task context <math id="algx1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algx1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="algx1.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CEFL:</span> Generate candidate pool <math id="algx1.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{t}\leftarrow\textsc{CEFL}(x_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">CEFL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{t}\leftarrow\textsc{CEFL}(x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="algx1.l6.4" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><math id="algx1.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math> Externally governed candidate generation
</span>
</div>
<div id="algx1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algx1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="algx1.l7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scoring:</span> For each <math id="algx1.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}\in C_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}\in C_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, compute scores <math id="algx1.l7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i,t}\leftarrow f_{\theta_{t-1}}(A_{i},x_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i,t}\leftarrow f_{\theta_{t-1}}(A_{i},x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algx1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="algx1.l8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hard Constraints:</span> Apply deterministic filters to obtain <math id="algx1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{C}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{C}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algx1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="algx1.l9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reducer:</span> Compute surfaced set <math id="algx1.l9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{t}\leftarrow\textsc{Reducer}(\tilde{C}_{t},\{s_{i,t}\},\phi_{t-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Reducer</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>C</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{t}\leftarrow\textsc{Reducer}(\tilde{C}_{t},\{s_{i,t}\},\phi_{t-1})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algx1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="algx1.l10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Selection:</span> Sample agent <math id="algx1.l10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="A_{k}\sim\pi_{\theta_{t-1}}(\cdot\mid x_{t},S_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{k}\sim\pi_{\theta_{t-1}}(\cdot\mid x_{t},S_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algx1.l11.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">11:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="algx1.l11.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Execution:</span> Produce output <math id="algx1.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{t}\leftarrow A_{k}(x_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{t}\leftarrow A_{k}(x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algx1.l12.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">12:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="algx1.l12.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Evaluation:</span> Obtain correctness signal <math id="algx1.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}\leftarrow E(o_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}\leftarrow E(o_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algx1.l13.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">13:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="algx1.l13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scoring Update:</span>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algx1.l14.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">14:</span></span>
              
              
                <math id="algx1.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta^{\prime}_{t}\leftarrow\theta_{t-1}+\alpha\,r_{t}\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta_{t-1}}(A_{k}\mid x_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta^{\prime}_{t}\leftarrow\theta_{t-1}+\alpha\,r_{t}\nabla_{\theta}\log\pi_{\theta_{t-1}}(A_{k}\mid x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algx1.l15.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">15:</span></span>
              
              
                <math id="algx1.l15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t}\leftarrow\Pi_{\mathcal{C}}(\theta^{\prime}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t}\leftarrow\Pi_{\mathcal{C}}(\theta^{\prime}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algx1.l16.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">16:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="algx1.l16.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reducer Update:</span>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l17" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algx1.l17.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">17:</span></span>
              
              
                <math id="algx1.l17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi^{\prime}_{t}\leftarrow\phi_{t-1}+\beta\,r_{t}\nabla_{\phi}L_{\mathrm{selection}}(\phi_{t-1};S_{t},\{s_{i,t}\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>ϕ</mi></msub><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>selection</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi^{\prime}_{t}\leftarrow\phi_{t-1}+\beta\,r_{t}\nabla_{\phi}L_{\mathrm{selection}}(\phi_{t-1};S_{t},\{s_{i,t}\})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l18" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algx1.l18.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">18:</span></span>
              
              
                <math id="algx1.l18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}\leftarrow\Pi_{\mathcal{G}}(\phi^{\prime}_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}\leftarrow\Pi_{\mathcal{G}}(\phi^{\prime}_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l19" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algx1.l19.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">19:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="algx1.l19.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Audit &amp; Logging:</span> Append <math id="algx1.l19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x_{t},C_{t},\{s_{i,t}\},S_{t},A_{k},o_{t},r_{t},\theta_{t},\phi_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x_{t},C_{t},\{s_{i,t}\},S_{t},A_{k},o_{t},r_{t},\theta_{t},\phi_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="algx1.l19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l20" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algx1.l20.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">20:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="algx1.l20.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span> projection clipped parameters <span id="algx1.l20.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">then</span>

</div>
<div id="algx1.l21" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algx1.l21.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">21:</span></span>
              
              
               Trigger fail-loud mechanism (BLOCK / THROTTLE / ALERT) and record event

</div>
<div id="algx1.l22" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algx1.l22.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">22:</span></span>
              
              
              <span id="algx1.l22.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="algx1.l22.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">if</span>
</div>
<div id="algx1.l23" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algx1.l23.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">23:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="algx1.l23.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="algx1.l23.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="algx1.l24" class="ltx_listingline">
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="algx1.l24.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">24:</span></span>
              
              
            <span id="algx1.l24.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="algx1.l24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The key difference from the static architecture is the introduction of the final stage, in which scoring and reducer parameters are updated using evaluative feedback, subject to projection onto governance constraint sets.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Candidate Expansion and Freezing Layer (CEFL)</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Candidate Expansion and Freezing Layer remains non-adaptive. Its role is to generate a candidate universe <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{t}\subseteq\mathcal{A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒜</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{t}\subseteq\mathcal{A}</annotation></semantics></math> for context <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> using externally defined rules and entropy sources.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">CEFL satisfies the following properties:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is non-agentic and has no learnable parameters.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It is externally governed and not influenced by reinforcement updates.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It guarantees non-zero exposure probability for admissible candidates.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">By keeping CEFL static, the architecture prevents reinforcement dynamics from collapsing the candidate universe itself.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">IV-C</span> </span><span id="S4.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Scoring Module with Learnable Weights</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each candidate <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{i}\in C_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{i}\in C_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is assigned a score vector:</p>
<table id="S4.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{i,t}=f_{\theta_{t}}(A_{i},x_{t})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{i,t}=f_{\theta_{t}}(A_{i},x_{t}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are learnable scoring parameters.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">These parameters may weight utility, risk, stability, or other domain-specific criteria. Reinforcement modifies <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, but updates are projected onto constraint set <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Sovereignty Constraint on Scoring.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Constraints encoded in <math id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> may include:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Minimum entropy of selection distribution.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Upper bound on concentration.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Mandatory diversity thresholds.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Projection ensures that reinforcement cannot eliminate structural exploration.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">IV-D</span> </span><span id="S4.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Governed Reducer with Adaptive Parameters</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Reducer applies deterministic filtering and stochastic selection using parameters <math id="S4.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Typical reducer components include:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Hard policy constraints,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Variance clamping,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Pareto frontier filtering,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I4.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I4.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Diversity bucketing,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I4.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I4.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I4.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Stochastic sampling.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In Selection as Power, certain reducer parameters (e.g., thresholds, exploration coefficients) become adaptive. However, these updates are projected onto feasible set <math id="S4.SS4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> to preserve invariants.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reducer Constraint Examples.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I5" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I5.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\max}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\max}</annotation></semantics></math> bounded below a fixed ceiling.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Exploration quota <math id="S4.I5.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k\geq k_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k\geq k_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I5.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I5.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I5.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Diversity buckets <math id="S4.I5.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\geq d_{\min}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>min</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\geq d_{\min}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This prevents the reducer from collapsing into deterministic top-1 selection under reinforcement.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS5.6" class="ltx_text">IV-E</span> </span><span id="S4.SS5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Evaluation Module</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After a candidate agent produces output <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, the evaluator <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> assigns reward <math id="S4.SS5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}\in\{-1,+1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}\in\{-1,+1\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We assume:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I6" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I6.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I6.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I6.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Evaluator correctness.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I6.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I6.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I6.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Deterministic reward assignment.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I6.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I6.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I6.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">No strategic interaction between evaluator and candidates.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS5.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This isolates selection dynamics from reward noise and allows analysis of reinforcement under ideal supervision.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS6.6" class="ltx_text">IV-F</span> </span><span id="S4.SS6.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Constrained Dual Update Mechanism</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Following evaluation, both <math id="S4.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are updated.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Scoring Update.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Parameters <math id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are updated via gradient ascent on reward-weighted log-likelihood, followed by projection onto <math id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reducer Update.</h5>

<div id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Parameters <math id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> are updated using reward-weighted gradients of a differentiable surrogate objective, then projected onto <math id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This dual-update design distinguishes our architecture from standard reinforcement pipelines, where either only policy parameters are updated or constraints are applied as penalties rather than hard projections.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS7.6" class="ltx_text">IV-G</span> </span><span id="S4.SS7.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">System Properties</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The architecture satisfies the following properties:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S4.I7" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S4.I7.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S4.I7.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I7.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I7.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bounded Selection Authority:</span> Selection concentration remains bounded by constraints encoded in <math id="S4.I7.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I7.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I7.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S4.I7.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I7.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I7.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Adaptive Reinforcement:</span> Correct selections increase selection probability within allowable bounds.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I7.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S4.I7.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I7.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I7.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fail-Loud Behavior:</span> Constraint violations trigger blocking or throttling.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS8" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS8.6" class="ltx_text">IV-H</span> </span><span id="S4.SS8.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Relation to Constrained Optimization</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Projection-based updates are standard in convex optimization and constrained learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. However, in this architecture, projections serve not merely to ensure feasibility, but to enforce institutional sovereignty invariants over selection authority.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS8.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS8.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Thus, reinforcement operates within a bounded feasible region that encodes governance rules, transforming selection governance into a constrained dynamical system.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS8.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS8.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The next section describes the experimental setup used to evaluate this architecture under adversarial and collusive conditions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">V </span><span id="S5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experimental Setup</span></h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section describes the experimental protocol used to evaluate incentivized selection governance under sequential adaptation and multi-agent dynamics. The goal of the evaluation is to assess empirical stability and bounded selection concentration under reinforcement updates.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">V-A</span> </span><span id="S5.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Sequential Evaluation Protocol</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike the static evaluation in Selection as Power, experiments here are conducted over temporal horizons of length <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math>. At each iteration:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S5.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A task context <math id="S5.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> is sampled from a stationary distribution.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A governed selection policy chooses an agent.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The selected agent produces output.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">4.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A perfect evaluator assigns reward <math id="S5.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{t}\in\{-1,+1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{t}\in\{-1,+1\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">5.</span> 
<div id="S5.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Scoring and reducer parameters are updated via constrained reinforcement.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Each experimental run consists of <math id="S5.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=250" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>250</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=250</annotation></semantics></math> iterations. All results are averaged across independent seeds.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">V-B</span> </span><span id="S5.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Domain Scenarios</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Experiments are conducted across three regulated financial scenarios:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S5.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Fraud Detection:</span> selection among fraud monitoring agents under heterogeneous fraud patterns.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Payments Infrastructure Monitoring:</span> anomaly detection across infrastructure monitoring agents.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">QBR Analysis:</span> financial summarization and risk reporting across analysis agents.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Each scenario includes five task variants, yielding heterogeneous contexts and stress-testing the selection policy under realistic domain variation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">V-C</span> </span><span id="S5.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Candidate Agent Pool</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The candidate set consists of <math id="S5.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=7</annotation></semantics></math> structured financial AI agents with heterogeneous feature profiles:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S5.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">risk profile,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">stability score,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">latency,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">auditability score,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I3.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I3.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I3.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">regulatory compliance tags.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">CEFL generates context-conditioned candidate subsets using deterministic embedding similarity (hash-based for reproducibility) with overshoot factor <math id="S5.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">V-D</span> </span><span id="S5.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Reinforcement Configuration</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Learning rates are set as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S5.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha\in\{0.01,0.05\},\qquad\beta\in\{0.01,0.05\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0.01</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.05</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>0.01</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>0.05</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha\in\{0.01,0.05\},\qquad\beta\in\{0.01,0.05\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Constraint sets <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> enforce:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S5.I4" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">minimum exploration probability <math id="S5.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\min}=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\min}=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I4.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I4.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I4.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">maximum selection concentration bound,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I4.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I4.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I4.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">minimum diversity buckets <math id="S5.I4.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d_{\min}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>d</mi><mi>min</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d_{\min}=2</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I4.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I4.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I4.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">variance clamp <math id="S5.I4.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{\max}=0.18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>max</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0.18</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{\max}=0.18</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Projection is performed after every update.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS5.6" class="ltx_text">V-E</span> </span><span id="S5.SS5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Baseline Comparisons</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare the incentivized governance architecture against:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S5.I5" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I5.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I5.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I5.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I5.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Static Governance:</span> no reinforcement updates.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I5.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I5.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I5.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I5.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unconstrained RL:</span> reinforcement without projection.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I5.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I5.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I5.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.I5.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scalar Top-K:</span> deterministic aggregation without diversity constraints.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">These baselines allow isolation of:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S5.I6" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S5.I6.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I6.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I6.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the effect of reinforcement,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I6.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I6.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I6.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the effect of sovereignty projection,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I6.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S5.I6.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I6.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">the effect of reducer adaptation.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS6.6" class="ltx_text">V-F</span> </span><span id="S5.SS6.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Metrics</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate five categories of metrics.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS6.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S5.SS6.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">V-F</span>1 </span>Selection Concentration</h4>

<div id="S5.SS6.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S5.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{SC}_{t}=\max_{i}P_{t}(A_{i})" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>SC</mtext><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><mi>i</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{SC}_{t}=\max_{i}P_{t}(A_{i})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We report temporal trajectories and final-period concentration.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS6.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S5.SS6.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">V-F</span>2 </span>Reinforced Selection Concentration (RSC)</h4>

<div id="S5.SS6.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Measures change in concentration over time:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS6.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S5.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{RSC}=\text{SC}_{T}-\text{SC}_{0}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>RSC</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>SC</mtext><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mtext>SC</mtext><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{RSC}=\text{SC}_{T}-\text{SC}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS6.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S5.SS6.SSS3.6" class="ltx_text">V-F</span>3 </span>Governance Stability Index (GSI)</h4>

<div id="S5.SS6.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S5.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{GSI}=1-\frac{\text{Var}(\text{SC}_{t})}{\text{Var}_{\text{unconstrained}}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>GSI</mtext><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mrow><mtext>Var</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mtext>SC</mtext><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><msub><mtext>Var</mtext><mtext>unconstrained</mtext></msub></mfrac></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{GSI}=1-\frac{\text{Var}(\text{SC}_{t})}{\text{Var}_{\text{unconstrained}}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS6.SSS4" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S5.SS6.SSS4.6" class="ltx_text">V-F</span>4 </span>Governance Debt (GD)</h4>

<div id="S5.SS6.SSS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS6.SSS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fraction of iterations triggering fail-loud safeguards.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS7.6" class="ltx_text">V-G</span> </span><span id="S5.SS7.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Evaluation Objectives</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The experiments are designed to test:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS7.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S5.I7" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S5.I7.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S5.I7.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I7.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Whether constrained reinforcement increases selection concentration.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I7.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S5.I7.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I7.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Whether projection bounds prevent collapse into deterministic dominance.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S5.I7.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">3.</span> 
<div id="S5.I7.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.I7.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Whether governance mechanisms preserve empirical stability.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VI </span><span id="S6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experimental Results</span></h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section reports the results of the dynamic learning-rate sweep experiments described in Section <a href="#S5" title="V Experimental Setup ‣ Selection as Power: Constrained Reinforcement for Bounded Decision Authority" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">V</span></a>. The analysis covers three financial scenarios and four governance architectures across two learning rates (<math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.01</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.01</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.05</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.05</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">VI-A</span> </span><span id="S6.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Temporal Evolution of Selection Concentration</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figures <a href="#S6.F1.sf1" title="In Fig. 1 ‣ VI-A Temporal Evolution of Selection Concentration ‣ VI Experimental Results ‣ Selection as Power: Constrained Reinforcement for Bounded Decision Authority" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(a)</span></a>–<a href="#S6.F1.sf3" title="In Fig. 1 ‣ VI-A Temporal Evolution of Selection Concentration ‣ VI Experimental Results ‣ Selection as Power: Constrained Reinforcement for Bounded Decision Authority" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(c)</span></a> show the temporal evolution of selection concentration <math id="S6.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{SC}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>SC</mtext><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{SC}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> across learning rates and architectures for each scenario.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S6.F1.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2603.02019v1/figures/fraud/sc_t_across_lrs_and_architectures.png" id="S6.F1.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/369;" width="476" height="369" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F1.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S6.F1.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fraud Detection</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S6.F1.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2603.02019v1/figures/payments/sc_t_across_lrs_and_architectures.png" id="S6.F1.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/369;" width="476" height="369" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F1.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S6.F1.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Payments Infrastructure Monitoring</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_3">
<figure id="S6.F1.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2603.02019v1/figures/qbr/sc_t_across_lrs_and_architectures.png" id="S6.F1.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/369;" width="476" height="369" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F1.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="S6.F1.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">QBR Analysis</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 1</span>: </span><span id="S6.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Temporal evolution of selection concentration
(<math id="S6.F1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SC}_{t}=\max_{i}P_{t}(A_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>SC</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>max</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SC}_{t}=\max_{i}P_{t}(A_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>) across governance architectures and learning rates.
Unconstrained reinforcement exhibits rapid concentration growth and, in some cases,
deterministic lock-in. Incentivized governance allows adaptive reinforcement while
preserving bounded selection authority through projection-based sovereignty constraints.
Higher learning rates accelerate reinforcement dynamics but remain constrained under governance.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Across all domains, unconstrained_rl exhibits rapid concentration growth, particularly under <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="lr=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">lr=0.05</annotation></semantics></math>. In fraud detection (Figure <a href="#S6.F1.sf1" title="In Fig. 1 ‣ VI-A Temporal Evolution of Selection Concentration ‣ VI Experimental Results ‣ Selection as Power: Constrained Reinforcement for Bounded Decision Authority" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(a)</span></a>), unconstrained_rl reaches <math id="S6.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{SC}_{T}=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mtext>SC</mtext><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{SC}_{T}=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>, indicating deterministic lock-in under repeated reinforcement.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, incentivized governance exhibits controlled reinforcement. Concentration increases over time but remains bounded below unity across scenarios. This bounded behavior is visible in Figures <a href="#S6.F1.sf1" title="In Fig. 1 ‣ VI-A Temporal Evolution of Selection Concentration ‣ VI Experimental Results ‣ Selection as Power: Constrained Reinforcement for Bounded Decision Authority" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(a)</span></a>, <a href="#S6.F1.sf2" title="In Fig. 1 ‣ VI-A Temporal Evolution of Selection Concentration ‣ VI Experimental Results ‣ Selection as Power: Constrained Reinforcement for Bounded Decision Authority" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(b)</span></a>, and <a href="#S6.F1.sf3" title="In Fig. 1 ‣ VI-A Temporal Evolution of Selection Concentration ‣ VI Experimental Results ‣ Selection as Power: Constrained Reinforcement for Bounded Decision Authority" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(c)</span></a>, where incentivized trajectories consistently remain below unconstrained reinforcement.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">VI-B</span> </span><span id="S6.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Learning Rate Effects</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S6.F2" title="Fig. 2 ‣ VI-B Learning Rate Effects ‣ VI Experimental Results ‣ Selection as Power: Constrained Reinforcement for Bounded Decision Authority" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> compares <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{SC}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>SC</mtext><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{SC}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> trajectories under <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="lr=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">lr=0.01</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="lr=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">lr=0.05</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.02019v1/figures/lr_comparison.png" id="S6.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/369;" width="476" height="369" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 2</span>: </span><span id="S6.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
Comparison of learning rates (<math id="S6.F2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.01</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.01</annotation></semantics></math> vs <math id="S6.F2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.05</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.05</annotation></semantics></math>) on the temporal evolution of selection concentration
(<math id="S6.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SC}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>SC</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SC}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>). Higher learning rates accelerate reinforcement dynamics, producing faster growth
in concentration. Under unconstrained reinforcement this leads to rapid lock-in, whereas incentivized
governance bounds concentration growth through projection-based sovereignty constraints.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Increasing the learning rate accelerates reinforcement dynamics:</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S6.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In unconstrained_rl, higher learning rates lead to faster lock-in and greater final concentration.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In incentivized mode, higher learning rates increase concentration but projection prevents deterministic dominance.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">This demonstrates that learning rate controls reinforcement velocity, while sovereignty projection bounds its magnitude.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">VI-C</span> </span><span id="S6.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Realized Selection Dominance (Top-Agent Share)</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While <math id="S6.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{SC}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>SC</mtext><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{SC}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> measures concentration of the selection distribution, the realized dominance of a single agent is better captured by the empirical selection frequency of the most selected agent, denoted <span id="S6.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">top_agent_share</span>. Figure <a href="#S6.F3" title="Fig. 3 ‣ VI-C Realized Selection Dominance (Top-Agent Share) ‣ VI Experimental Results ‣ Selection as Power: Constrained Reinforcement for Bounded Decision Authority" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> plots this metric over time for each architecture and learning rate.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.02019v1/figures/top_share.png" id="S6.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:405/95;" width="405" height="95" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. 3</span>: </span><span id="S6.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Realized cumulative top-agent selection share across scenarios and governance architectures. Each panel reports the fraction of selections assigned to the most frequently chosen agent as a function of successful steps. Unconstrained reinforcement learning exhibits monotonic convergence toward deterministic dominance, with higher learning rates accelerating lock-in. Scalar top-<math id="S6.F3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> produces structurally high dominance from early iterations due to deterministic aggregation. In contrast, incentivized governance increases selection preference for high-performing agents while stabilizing below deterministic collapse through projection-based sovereignty constraints. Static mode remains approximately constant, reflecting the absence of learning.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S6.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The patterns are structurally distinct:</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S6.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S6.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Unconstrained RL.</span> Realized dominance increases monotonically and approaches <math id="S6.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.0</annotation></semantics></math> under both learning rates, with <math id="S6.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="lr=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">lr=0.05</annotation></semantics></math> converging more rapidly. This confirms deterministic lock-in: once an agent accumulates bias, reinforcement amplifies it without bound.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scalar_topk.</span> Dominance is high and stable from early iterations. Because the reducer is deterministic and diversity is absent, selection frequency reflects structural monoculture rather than learned adaptation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Incentivized Governance.</span> Dominance increases gradually under reinforcement but stabilizes strictly below unity. Under <math id="S6.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="lr=0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">lr=0.01</annotation></semantics></math>, top-agent share rises slowly and plateaus at moderate levels. Under <math id="S6.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="lr=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">lr=0.05</annotation></semantics></math>, growth is faster but remains bounded, never reaching deterministic collapse.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S6.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S6.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Static.</span> Selection frequency remains flat and low, reflecting absence of learning.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">These trajectories reveal a key distinction: projection constraints do not prevent reinforcement-driven preference formation, but they prevent irreversible dominance. Incentivized governance allows adaptive bias accumulation while maintaining residual competition among agents.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">VI-D</span> </span><span id="S6.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Governance Debt Trade-off</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Under higher learning rates, incentivized governance incurs measurable governance debt (GD_dynamic), reflecting projection clipping events when reinforcement attempts to exceed concentration constraints.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This trade-off is visible in Figure <a href="#S6.F1.sf2" title="In Fig. 1 ‣ VI-A Temporal Evolution of Selection Concentration ‣ VI Experimental Results ‣ Selection as Power: Constrained Reinforcement for Bounded Decision Authority" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(b)</span></a>, where incentivized trajectories approach concentration bounds but are periodically stabilized by projection.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS5.6" class="ltx_text">VI-E</span> </span><span id="S6.SS5.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Task-Level Reinforcement Dynamics</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The top_share plots further show that reinforcement effects are most visible under repeated task contexts. In unconstrained_rl, repeated reward signals quickly drive near-exclusive selection of a single agent. In incentivized governance, repeated contexts still reinforce high-performing agents, but concentration stabilizes at sub-deterministic levels.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Importantly, the gap between unconstrained_rl and incentivized modes widens with higher learning rates. Under <math id="S6.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="lr=0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">lr=0.05</annotation></semantics></math>, unconstrained reinforcement collapses rapidly, whereas incentivized reinforcement accelerates but remains bounded due to projection clipping.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This empirical divergence confirms that sovereignty projection regulates not only distribution-level concentration (as measured by <math id="S6.SS5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{SC}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mtext>SC</mtext><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{SC}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>), but also realized selection dominance over time.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS6.6" class="ltx_text">VI-F</span> </span><span id="S6.SS6.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Scenario-Level Metric Summary</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S6.T1" title="TABLE I ‣ VI-F Scenario-Level Metric Summary ‣ VI Experimental Results ‣ Selection as Power: Constrained Reinforcement for Bounded Decision Authority" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">I</span></a> summarizes the main evaluation metrics across scenarios, governance architectures, and learning rates. Metrics include mean reward, average selection concentration (mean_SC), initial and final concentration (<math id="S6.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SC}_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>SC</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SC}_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{SC}_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>SC</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{SC}_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>), reinforced selection concentration (RSC), temporal variance of concentration (var_SC), and dynamic governance debt (GD_dynamic).</p>
</div>
